# <Title>

> **This is the governing specification.** AI and implementers read *this*, not the raw
> requirement document. If it is wrong or stale, fix it here — do not work around it from the
> source. A `source_hash` mismatch means the requirement changed underneath this file; CI fails
> until it is re-derived.

## Scope

One paragraph: what question this spec answers, and — just as importantly — what it does not
cover. Name the adjacent specs that own the rest.

## Normative rules

The actual content. Numbered so they can be cited (`SPEC-NNN §R3`) from PRDs, ADRs, test cases,
and commit messages.

Write rules as **statements of required behaviour**, not narrative. A reader must be able to
answer the governing question from this section alone, without opening the source.

- **R1** — …
- **R2** — …

## Reference detail

Tables, enumerations, and mappings the rules depend on. Where the converted source already
carries a well-formed table, cite it by section rather than copying — but only when the citation
is precise enough that a reader does not have to search:

> Full per-report column mapping: `<source>` § `<section>`.

Copy the table here instead whenever it is short, frequently needed, or the source section is
sprawling.

## Known gaps / deliberate omissions

Anything the source leaves undefined, contradicts elsewhere, or defers. **Say so explicitly** —
an unstated gap gets silently invented by whoever reads this next.

